# .context: handoff from the grilling session

Point your Claude at this file at the start of the next session. Grilling is finished; the next step is `to-spec`.

## Your job in the next session
1. Read `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `CONTEXT.md` (the glossary — use its terms exactly) and `docs/adr/`.
2. Run `/to-spec` (Opus) to write `docs/specs/storm-shield.md` from the decisions below. Then `/to-tickets`; tickets 0001–0003 build the cut line end to end.
3. After the spec, fill in Idea / Stack / Commands in `AGENTS.md` once and push once (only the user pushes).
4. **Scope rule:** judges only see a 3-minute demo. Build what shows on stage; big upgrades are stretch tickets ordered last.
5. No AI attribution in commits or PRs.

## The project: Storm Shield
A neighborhood disaster dashboard for Gainesville / Alachua County, FL. Community sensor readings are shared over a local mesh, so residents keep seeing conditions and their Evacuation Plan when the internet goes down. Adds hyperlocal ground truth next to NWS/NHC/county; never replaces them.

## Decisions (grilling, 2026-10-16)

| # | Question | Decision |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Name | **Storm Shield** (was "Beacon Mesh"); same functionality. |
| 2 | Hero hazard | Hurricane approach is the setup (falling pressure); flood is the climax. |
| 3 | Hardware | One Raspberry Pi 4, a breadboard and wires. No ESP32, no LoRa, no real sensors. |
| 4 | Team split | Assigned per ticket as tickets arrive. |
| 5 | Cameras | Public cameras, online only; tiles grey out when internet drops. Exact source TBD in spec. |
| 6 | Dashboard audience | One screen: shared map and readings + an Evacuation Plan panel personalized to a Household Profile. |
| 7 | Cut line | One Node → dashboard over the mesh → unplug internet → readings keep updating → cached Evacuation Plan renders. Build this first. |
| 8 | Mesh members | The Pi (breadboard Sensor Node) + 4 teammates' laptops running software Sensor Nodes. Phones are Viewers, not Nodes. |
| 9 | Mesh software | **Reticulum** over Wi-Fi from ticket one (ADR 0001). |
| 10 | Breadboard | Digital GPIO buttons/switches step a Scenario that produces Simulated Readings (Pi has no analog input). |
| 11 | Who says "evacuate" | Only official sources. The Hub sends signed **Community Warnings**, labeled non-official, always pointing to official sources for evacuation. |
| 12 | Hub location | The Hub is a Node on the mesh, also pulling official feeds and the Cache Pack while online (ADR 0002). |
| 13 | Flood Risk where | Every Node computes Flood Risk locally; only the Hub sends Community Warnings. |
| 14 | Flood Risk how | Transparent rules → Low / Elevated / High, with reasons shown. Hub warns only when 2+ Registered Nodes are High. |
| 15 | Trust | Only Registered Nodes (identity accepted by the Hub at sign-up) count. Unverified Nodes shown greyed, never counted. |
| 16 | Personal inputs | Household Profile: home location, housing type (site-built vs mobile home/RV), special-needs, pets. Stored only on the device. |
| 17 | Offline routing | Routes precomputed into the Cache Pack (grid of points → every Shelter). On-device rerouting = low-priority stretch. |
| 18 | Official Alerts on demo day | Labeled replay of Hurricane Helene (Sept 2024): real NWS alerts + Alachua County order. Same code path as live. |
| 19 | Stack | Python (FastAPI) per Node + React/Vite/TypeScript + Leaflet dashboard. uv and pnpm. |
| 20 | Cache Pack delivery | Each Node downloads the full pack while online; only small updates (Shelter status, replayed alerts) cross the mesh. |
| 21 | Privacy | Node locations fuzzed to ~200 m before sharing. |
| 22 | Local network | The Pi runs its own Wi-Fi hotspot (no internet). Hub laptop gets internet via a USB-tethered phone; "internet off" = unplug that cable on stage. |
| 23 | Central dashboard | Same app on every Node; the Hub adds a Node registry and Community Warning log. |
| 24 | Demo storyline | See below. |

### Demo storyline (3 minutes)
1. Normal day: Hub dashboard shows 5 Registered Nodes, live readings, Flood Risk Low, public camera tiles.
2. Storm approaching: "pressure drop" button → pressure falls across Nodes, storm signal on the map.
3. Official Alert: replayed Helene NWS alert, labeled OFFICIAL · REPLAY.
4. Flood risk rises: "rain" switch + "water rising" button → 2 Nodes High → Hub sends a signed Community Warning.
5. Pull the cable: internet off, camera tiles grey, readings keep flowing over the mesh.
6. Mesh heals: close one laptop Node (never the Pi — it is the network); everything else keeps working.
7. Offline plan: resident's phone with a mobile-home Household Profile shows Flood Risk High, the replayed county order, nearest open Shelter and precomputed route, zero internet.

## Research findings to use in the spec
- **Evacuation in Alachua:** no lettered zones. County orders target mobile/manufactured homes and RVs, unsafe housing, "areas subject to flooding" and homes near water (Debby, Helene, Idalia). Approximate Flood-Prone Areas with FEMA NFHL + Hogtown Creek / Sweetwater Branch floodplains. Helene order: https://alachuacounty.us/News/Article/Pages/Hurricane-Helene-Update-11-–-Evacuation-Order-in-Effect.aspx
- **Shelters:** ~20 pre-identified sites (mostly schools), one special-needs (Senior Recreation Center), one pet-friendly; announced per storm. FEMA open shelters: https://gis.fema.gov/arcgis/rest/services/NSS/OpenShelters/FeatureServer/0 . FDEM Alachua shelter table (2018): https://prod.floridadisaster.org/globalassets/dem/response/sesp/2018/counties/alachua-county.pdf
- **Gauges:** no active USGS gauge on Hogtown or Sweetwater (our Nodes would be the only live creek data). Live Santa Fe River gauges: 02321975, 02321958, 02321500, 02320700. Cedar Key tide 8727520.
- **Feeds:** NWS alerts `https://api.weather.gov/alerts/active?zone=FLZ136` (User-Agent required); NHC `https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/CurrentStorms.json`; roads from Geofabrik Florida OSM extract.
- **Reticulum:** Python stack (`pip install rns`), AutoInterface discovers peers on the same LAN; Node identities give signatures for free.

## Deferred (tracked, not blocking the spec)
The spec names these as open and moves on; don't stall `to-spec` on them.
- **Public camera source:** any free, accessible public feed (FL511 / FDOT, Flock, etc.). Pick it when the camera ticket comes up.
- **Message formats:** Community Reading, Community Warning, Shelter status update, Cache Pack layout. Decide them in the UI design doc that follows the spec, since they're designed with the dashboard. Changes after that need everyone.
- **Flood Risk thresholds** (Low / Elevated / High): set once the Node backend and its sensor types exist.

## Team workflow (4 people)
Branch per ticket `NNNN-<slug>` off up-to-date `main`; merge yourself. Only the user runs `to-tickets` and fills `AGENTS.md`. Shared formats live in the spec. Stay in your feature folder. Rejected push → `git pull --rebase` and retry.

